WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.357
Day 25 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.907 --> 00:00:09.240
A model for performance testing (1): the metrics that matter for an LLM.

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:14.881
The performance model of an LLM feature: workload profile, text-generation-specific

00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:16.782
metrics and percentile targets.

00:00:18.082 --> 00:00:22.695
Someone coming from traditional performance testing arrives at an endpoint with a

00:00:22.695 --> 00:00:27.251
model and applies what they know: requests per second, average latency and error

00:00:27.251 --> 00:00:27.535
rate.

00:00:27.835 --> 00:00:31.147
The numbers come out, and they describe the system poorly.

00:00:31.447 --> 00:00:35.797
Two requests to the same endpoint can generate twenty tokens or two thousand, and

00:00:35.797 --> 00:00:37.301
take half a second or forty.

00:00:37.601 --> 00:00:40.494
Averaging them blends things that aren't alike.

00:00:41.794 --> 00:00:44.200
A model's unit of work is the token.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:49.534
The user's experience depends on when the first one arrives and on how smoothly the

00:00:49.534 --> 00:00:50.261
rest follow.

00:00:50.561 --> 00:00:55.093
This guide introduces the metrics that describe that, how to define targets per

00:00:55.093 --> 00:00:58.134
percentile and how to measure a single-user baseline.

00:00:58.434 --> 00:01:02.813
Without that baseline, any later load test is a number with no reference.

00:01:04.113 --> 00:01:05.811
To follow this guide you need:

00:01:06.361 --> 00:01:09.893
An OpenAI-compatible endpoint (local Ollama works).

00:01:10.443 --> 00:01:12.861
Python 3.10+.

00:01:14.161 --> 00:01:15.440
This is the setup.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:19.957
Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.

00:01:21.257 --> 00:01:23.524
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:24.824 --> 00:01:25.883
The metrics.

00:01:26.433 --> 00:01:30.430
TTFT (time to first token): time until the first token.

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:33.228
It's what the user feels as "it answered".

00:01:33.778 --> 00:01:37.252
ITL (inter-token latency): time between tokens.

00:01:37.552 --> 00:01:40.307
It measures how smooth streaming text is.

00:01:40.857 --> 00:01:44.017
Output tokens/s: generation speed.

00:01:44.317 --> 00:01:46.607
It's the system's real capacity.

00:01:47.157 --> 00:01:52.373
Total latency p95 and p99: the tail is what generates complaints.

00:01:52.923 --> 00:01:58.173
Error rate (429, 5xx, timeouts): quota limits and saturation.

00:01:58.723 --> 00:02:02.754
Goodput: percentage of requests meeting all targets at once.

00:02:03.304 --> 00:02:06.662
Cost per request: input and output tokens.

00:02:06.962 --> 00:02:08.613
Load is paid for too.

00:02:09.913 --> 00:02:10.949
Step 1.

00:02:11.249 --> 00:02:13.272
Why classic metrics mislead.

00:02:13.822 --> 00:02:18.949
Understand why classic metrics mislead: two requests to the same endpoint can

00:02:18.949 --> 00:02:20.814
generate 20 or 2,000 tokens.

00:02:21.114 --> 00:02:25.227
Requests per second and average latency blend different things.

00:02:25.777 --> 00:02:30.737
In a traditional API, requests to the same endpoint cost roughly the same.

00:02:31.037 --> 00:02:36.225
With a model, each request's cost depends on how many tokens go in and, above all,

00:02:36.225 --> 00:02:39.515
how many come out, because generation is sequential.

00:02:39.815 --> 00:02:44.521
Ten requests per second with short answers and with long answers are two completely

00:02:44.521 --> 00:02:46.563
different loads for the same server.

00:02:46.863 --> 00:02:51.559
If you report only requests per second and average latency, two tests with the same

00:02:51.559 --> 00:02:53.936
result may have measured different things.

00:02:55.236 --> 00:02:56.434
Step 2.

00:02:56.734 --> 00:02:58.351
The workload profile.

00:02:58.901 --> 00:03:04.606
Define the workload profile: prompt sizes, expected output length, streaming or not,

00:03:04.606 --> 00:03:06.032
and peak concurrency.

00:03:06.582 --> 00:03:09.231
Before measuring, describe the real load.

00:03:09.531 --> 00:03:12.553
Which prompt sizes arrive and in what proportion.

00:03:12.853 --> 00:03:14.968
What answer length is expected.

00:03:15.268 --> 00:03:18.638
Whether streaming is used or the full answer is awaited.

00:03:18.938 --> 00:03:20.705
What peak concurrency is.

00:03:21.005 --> 00:03:25.821
Take this from production logs if they exist, or estimate it with the product team

00:03:25.821 --> 00:03:26.232
if not.

00:03:26.532 --> 00:03:30.668
A profile with three or four request types and their proportions is enough.

00:03:30.968 --> 00:03:35.947
Without a profile you'll test with an arbitrary prompt and get numbers that predict

00:03:35.947 --> 00:03:36.427
nothing.

00:03:37.727 --> 00:03:38.810
Step 3.

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:40.924
Targets per percentile.

00:03:41.474 --> 00:03:48.182
Set targets per percentile, not average: for example TTFT p95, total latency p95 and

00:03:48.182 --> 00:03:49.859
a maximum error rate.

00:03:50.409 --> 00:03:54.614
An average hides the tail, and the tail is where the complaining users are.

00:03:54.914 --> 00:03:59.623
Define targets as percentiles: for example, time to first token at the ninety-fifth

00:03:59.623 --> 00:04:04.274
percentile, total latency at the ninety-fifth percentile and a maximum error rate.

00:04:04.574 --> 00:04:09.143
The values come from the experience the product needs, not from what the system

00:04:09.143 --> 00:04:10.010
delivers today.

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:11.973
Write them before measuring.

00:04:12.273 --> 00:04:16.618
If you define them afterwards, they'll look suspiciously like what you measured.

00:04:17.918 --> 00:04:18.931
Step 4.

00:04:19.231 --> 00:04:21.091
The single-user baseline.

00:04:21.641 --> 00:04:24.546
Measure the single-user baseline with the script.

00:04:24.846 --> 00:04:28.750
If you miss targets with one user, there's no load test to run yet.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:34.384
Run the script with a single user and no concurrency, several times for each request

00:04:34.384 --> 00:04:35.595
type in the profile.

00:04:35.895 --> 00:04:40.333
That gives you the best case: what the system does when competing with nobody.

00:04:40.633 --> 00:04:45.334
If you miss targets with one user, there's no load test to run yet, because the

00:04:45.334 --> 00:04:50.155
problem lies in the chosen model, the prompt size or the infrastructure, and load

00:04:50.155 --> 00:04:51.583
will only make it worse.

00:04:51.883 --> 00:04:55.950
The baseline is also the reference for reading everything that comes next.

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:58.378
Step 5.

00:04:58.678 --> 00:04:59.598
Goodput.

00:05:00.148 --> 00:05:04.679
Add goodput: the percentage of requests meeting all targets at once.

00:05:05.229 --> 00:05:10.119
Goodput is the percentage of requests meeting all targets at once: first token on

00:05:10.119 --> 00:05:13.138
time, total latency within the limit and no error.

00:05:13.438 --> 00:05:18.328
It matters because the separate metrics can look fine while the experience is poor.

00:05:18.628 --> 00:05:23.428
A system can sustain many requests per second and have low goodput if a good share

00:05:23.428 --> 00:05:24.598
of them arrive late.

00:05:24.898 --> 00:05:30.002
It's the number that best sums up whether load is being served acceptably, and it's

00:05:30.002 --> 00:05:32.215
useful for comparing configurations.

00:05:33.515 --> 00:05:34.702
Step 6.

00:05:35.002 --> 00:05:36.479
Cost per request.

00:05:37.029 --> 00:05:39.006
Record cost per request.

00:05:39.306 --> 00:05:42.954
A load test against a paid API consumes real budget.

00:05:43.504 --> 00:05:47.802
Record each request's input and output tokens and convert them into cost.

00:05:48.102 --> 00:05:49.475
It has two uses.

00:05:49.775 --> 00:05:54.738
The first is product-related: knowing what it costs to serve a user at the expected

00:05:54.738 --> 00:05:55.037
load.

00:05:55.337 --> 00:05:57.000
The second is practical.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:02.247
A load test against a paid API consumes real budget, and a badly sized test can

00:06:02.247 --> 00:06:05.127
spend in an hour what was planned for a month.

00:06:05.427 --> 00:06:09.551
Estimate the test's cost before running it and use a key with a spending cap.

00:06:10.851 --> 00:06:11.957
An example.

00:06:12.507 --> 00:06:17.566
With illustrative values: a team defines three request types for its assistant,

00:06:17.566 --> 00:06:21.345
short, medium and long, in a sixty, thirty, ten proportion.

00:06:21.645 --> 00:06:26.333
The targets are first token in under a second and a half and total latency under

00:06:26.333 --> 00:06:29.380
twelve seconds, both at the ninety-fifth percentile.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:33.549
The single-user baseline shows the short and medium ones comply.

00:06:33.849 --> 00:06:38.804
The long ones take eighteen seconds in total, even though the first token arrives on

00:06:38.804 --> 00:06:39.099
time.

00:06:39.399 --> 00:06:42.292
Before any load test there's already a finding.

00:06:42.592 --> 00:06:47.099
They discuss it with product and agree to limit the length of those answers, which

00:06:47.099 --> 00:06:48.748
brings them to eleven seconds.

00:06:50.048 --> 00:06:51.711
The most common mistakes.

00:06:52.261 --> 00:06:55.875
Reporting only requests per second and average latency.

00:06:56.425 --> 00:07:00.003
Testing with a single prompt that doesn't represent the real load.

00:07:00.553 --> 00:07:02.971
Setting targets after seeing the measurements.

00:07:03.521 --> 00:07:07.204
Launching a load test without estimating what it will cost.

00:07:08.504 --> 00:07:10.747
To know it is done, check this.

00:07:11.297 --> 00:07:14.632
The workload profile has request types and proportions.

00:07:15.182 --> 00:07:18.087
Targets are written per percentile before measuring.

00:07:18.637 --> 00:07:21.658
There is a single-user baseline for each request type.

00:07:22.208 --> 00:07:25.926
Each measurement includes tokens and cost per request.

00:07:27.226 --> 00:07:29.121
And where AI fell short.

00:07:29.671 --> 00:07:33.923
Assistants default to classic API metrics (RPS, averages).

00:07:34.223 --> 00:07:37.360
You have to explicitly ask for generation metrics.

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:40.462
That was day 25.

00:07:40.762 --> 00:07:45.737
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:07:45.737 --> 00:07:46.744
asked questions.

00:07:47.294 --> 00:07:53.148
